## 3. $H_{2}$ Binding to Metal Species
As the simplest known chemical compound, the hydrogen molecule has been the subject of countless experiments and theoretical investigations, leading to some of the most fundamental discoveries in the areas of electronic structure and chemical bonding. Although metal complexes with other relatively unreactive small molecules, such as ${\bf N}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{C}{=}\mathrm{CH}_{2}$ , and even $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , had been known for many years,[6] the first metal complex of an $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecule was not isolated until 1984, when Kubas and co-workers reported the now famous “Kubas complex” $\mathrm{[W(CO)}_{3}(\mathrm{P}i\mathrm{Pr}_{3})(\mathrm{H}_{2})]$ $i\mathrm{Pr}=$ isopropyl).[7] Using single-crystal neutron diffraction and a variety of other techniques, they later showed that this complex contained a side-on bound $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ ligand with an $\mathrm{H}{-}\mathrm{H}$ distance only slightly elongated relative to that in gaseous $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , thus unequivocally proving that the complex was not a classical dihydride.[8] Subsequent to this seminal discovery, $\mathbf{\sigma}_{\mathbf{{0}}-\mathbf{{H}}_{2}}$ complexes of virtually every transition metal have been reported, and their properties and reactivity have been the subject of many excellent review articles and a comprehensive book by Kubas.[9]
Surprisingly, despite the large number of $\mathbf{\sigma}_{\mathbf{{0}}-\mathbf{{H}}_{2}}$ complexes that have been reported so far, the vast literature on the subject contains very few studies that address the thermodynamic properties of the metal– $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}$ interaction, and in particular the bond dissociation energy (BDE) of the metal– $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}$ bond. Vibrational spectroscopy, variable-temperature NMR spectroscopy, or photoacoustic calorimetry have been used to quantify this interaction in only a handful of organometallic complexes (Table 1). The BDE values obtained for many of these complexes are affected by the fleeting formation of solvento species or by the presence of agostic $\mathrm{C-H}$ interactions. For example, formation of the transient complex $[(\mathrm{C_{6}H_{5}M e})\mathrm{Cr(CO)}_{2}(\mathrm{Xe})]$ allowed only an approximate measurement of the energy of dissociation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ from $[(\mathrm{C_{6}H_{5}M e})\mathrm{Cr(CO)}_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2})]$ , which was estimated at $70\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ .[27] The energy of the agostic $\mathrm{C-H}$ interaction, which affects particularly the complexes containing trialkylphosphine ligands, is also notoriously difficult to measure, and BDE values obtained for the respective $\mathbf{\sigma}_{\mathbf{{0}}-\mathbf{{H}}_{2}}$ complexes are likely underestimated by around $40\ensuremath{\mathrm{kJ}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{mol}}^{-1}$ .[9b]
Somewhat counterintuitively, the gas-phase measurements also revealed that the BDE typically increases from $n=1$ to $n=2$ for the transition-metal ions. This initial increase has been attributed to the mixing between the 3ds orbital and the empty 4s orbital, which is already present in the $\begin{array}{r}{[\mathbf{M}(\mathbf{H}_{2})^{+}]}\end{array}$ species. The typically linear geometry of the $\left[\mathbf{M}(\mathrm{H}_{2})_{2}^{+}\right]$ species allows both $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ ligands to share the cost of the hybridization, giving a larger BDE to the second $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecule.[29] A mild decrease in the BDE is observed with further increase of $n$ for all transition-metal ions, which has been associated with a decrease in the s-accepting abilities of the polyhydrogenated metal species. Importantly, this trend also suggests that unsaturated metal sites within metal– organic frameworks, which are typically surrounded by three, four, or five ligands of better $\upsigma$ -donating ability than $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , are expected to exhibit even lower $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ affinities than the respective $\left[\mathbf{M}(\mathrm{H}_{2})_{3}^{+}\right]$ , $\mathrm{[M(H}_{2})_{4}^{+}\mathrm{]}$ , and $\mathrm{[M(H}_{2})_{5}{}^{+}\mathrm{]}$ species.
In addition to the studies of molecular metal– $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}$ species, a large body of literature is dedicated to the interaction of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ with metal surfaces. Although molecular hydrogen is typically short-lived and dissociates at the surface of most metals, low temperature experiments and direct calorimetric measurements have allowed the characterization of a relatively large number of these fleeting interactions. Reported values for the enthalpy of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption to metal surfaces range from 39 and $42\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ for ${\mathrm{Cu}}(311)$ and $\mathrm{Pt}(111)$ , respectively, to 142 and $155\mathrm{kJmol^{-1}}$ for the (110) and (111) surfaces of Mo and W, respectively.[47]
Perhaps the most relevant experimental BDE values for the design of new frameworks for hydrogen storage have come only recently with the measurement of metal– $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}$ interactions in ion-exchanged zeolites.[12,13,15] Temperaturedependent infrared spectroscopy was used to determine the interaction energy of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ with ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ , $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ , and $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ ions in zeolites ZSM-5 and ferrierite, and showed that $\mathrm{{Na^{+}}}$ consistently binds $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ more strongly than do $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ and $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ . On the other hand, the study also suggests that none of these cations could provide the $13{-}15\mathrm{kJmol^{-1}}$ necessary for room-temperature hydrogen storage. However, the fact that $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ -exchanged zeolite Y showed an $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption enthalpy of $18\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ suggests that use of cations with no back-donation abilities, but with higher formal charges increases the electrostatic interaction with $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and may lead to materials with optimal $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energies.
Although limited, the data summarized in Table 1 allow several important conclusions to be made regarding metal- $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding for hydrogen storage. First, incorporation of alkalimetal cations in metal–organic frameworks is unlikely to lead to materials with $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energies fit for room-temperature storage. Instead, the experimental data show that the transition metals display a much wider range of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ affinities, some of which fall in the desired $15{-}20\mathrm{kJmol^{-1}}$ range. Moreover, gas-phase measurements for monovalent cations indicate that one could benefit especially from the use of $\mathrm{Co^{+}}$ $\mathbf{N}\mathbf{i}^{+}$ , and $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ species, which display stronger initial $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energies than other ions. Importantly, however, data reported for organometallic complexes suggest that the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energy characteristic of low-valent metals is too high for room-temperature applications and that novel means of incorporating metals with higher oxidation states and diminished back-donation abilities need to be found. In addition, the design of metal–organic frameworks with coordinatively unsaturated metal centers must take into account the weight of the metals used, thereby narrowing the available candidates to first-row transition metals and light, high-valent main-group cations such as $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ , ${\bf A l}^{3+}$ , and $\mathrm{Ga}^{3+}$ .
In the absence of a more extended pool of experimental results, theoretical investigations of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding to metals could also contribute significantly to the design of new hydrogen storage materials. Numerous computational studies have already modeled $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ interactions with single metal ions, metal surfaces, and organometallic $\mathbf{0}{\cdot}\mathbf{H}_{2}$ complexes.[48] For example, Lochan and Head-Gordon recently used DFT calculations to address the issue of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding to gas-phase $\mathrm{Li^{+},N a^{+},M g^{2+}}$ , and $\mathbf{Al}^{3+}$ ions and found that the strength of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding increases with increasing charge owing to the electrostatic interactions.[49] Computational investigations have also unveiled new structures that could exhibit excellent $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage properties, such as alkali-metal- and Ti-decorated fullerenes,[50] and even Li-doped metal–organic frameworks.[51] Synthesis of such materials, however, is likely to be difficult because of the expected bulk phase instability of many of the proposed structures.
Computational studies that model the metal– $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}$ interaction in metal–organic frameworks are also exceedingly scarce and have thus far relied mostly on grand canonical Monte Carlo simulations and ab initio calculations.[52] The difficulty of dealing with these complex solid-state systems is accentuated by the fact that open-shell electronic configurations and negative charge considerations often make DFT calculations computationally demanding and unreliable. As such, the issue of modeling the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ interaction with coordinatively unsaturated metal centers within metal–organic frameworks still represents an important and largely unsolved problem.
## 4. Metal–Hydrogen Binding in Metal–Organic Frameworks
Infrared spectroscopy provided the first experimental evidence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding to a metal center inside a metal– organic framework. Bordiga, Zecchina, and co-workers showed that $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorbed into $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{3}(\mathrm{{btc})_{2}}$ displays an infrared stretching band at $4100\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which is characteristic of metal– $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ interactions.[53] Low-temperature powder neutron diffraction experiments later verified that ${\bf D}_{2}$ binds to the empty, axial coordination sites of the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}$ –tetracarboxylate paddlewheel building units (Figure 1), and showed that the $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}{-}\mathbf{D}_{2}$ distance is $2.{\overset{-}{3}}9{\overset{\circ}{\mathrm{A}}}$ .[54] This distance is somewhat longer than the $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{n}}^{2+}-\ensuremath{\mathbf{D}}_{2}$ distance of $2.27\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ in $\mathrm{Mn}_{3}[(\mathrm{Mn}_{4}\mathrm{Cl})_{3}(\mathrm{btt})_{8}-$ - $\mathrm{(CH_{3}O H)_{10}}]_{2}$ (Figure 2), for which ${\bf D}_{2}$ binding to the squareplanar $\bf{M n}_{4}C l$ units was also probed by powder neutron diffraction.[55] The difference between the two $\mathbf{M}^{2+}\mathrm{-}\mathbf{D}_{2}$ distances agrees well with the observation that $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binds more strongly in the $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{2+}$ compound than in the $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ compound, which show zero-coverage enthalpies of adsorption of 10.1 and $6.8\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ , respectively.[55,56]
Metal– $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}$ interactions in microporous frameworks have been directly observed in only five other cases so far, and the hydrogen storage properties of these materials are also summarized in Table 2. For example, powder neutron diffraction experiments were employed to detect $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}{-\mathbf{D}_{2}}$ interactions in the Prussian blue analogue $\mathrm{Cu_{3}[C o(C N)_{6}]_{2}[^{64}}$ and in $\mathrm{HCu}[(\mathrm{Cu}_{4}\mathrm{Cl})_{3}(\mathrm{btt})_{8}]{\cdot}3.5\mathrm{HCl},^{[65]}$ a sodalite-type framework isostructural with the $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{n}}^{2+}$ compound displayed in Figure 2. Whereas the unsaturated ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ sites in the Prussian blue analogue were observed to bind ${\bf D}_{2}$ only at increased ${\bf D}_{2}$ loading, five-coordinate ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ions were identified as the strongest adsorption sites in the sodalite-type framework, in which $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}{-}\mathbf{D}_{2}$ distances of $2.47\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ were observed. As in $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{3}(\mathrm{{btc})_{2}}$ , the Jahn–Teller effect is likely to be responsible for this somewhat longer $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}{-}\mathbf{D}_{2}$ distance and the lower zerocoverage $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding enthalpy of $9.5\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ relative to the $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{n}}^{2+}$ analogue. However, in contrast to $\mathrm{{\bfMn}}_{3}[(\mathrm{{\bfMn}}_{4}\mathrm{{\bfCl}})_{3}(\mathrm{btt})_{8}.$ - $\mathrm{(CH_{3}O H)_{10}}]_{2}$ , for which methanol molecules occupy approximately $80\%$ of the $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{n}}^{2+}$ sites, neutron diffraction confirmed that all of the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ sites were available for ${\bf D}_{2}$ binding in $\mathrm{HCu}[(\mathrm{Cu}_{4}\mathrm{Cl})_{3}(\mathrm{btt})_{8}]{\cdot}3.5\mathrm{HCl}$ . The complete desolvation of the $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ sites resulted in a larger enthalpy of adsorption over the entire dosing pressure range, such that the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ analogue is expected to desorb $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ at a higher temperature than the $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{n}}^{2+}$ analogue.[65]
A very recent neutron diffraction experiment has also allowed the identification of $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}{\mathrm{-H}}_{2}$ interactions within the microporous framework $\mathrm{Zn_{2}(d h t p)}$ (dhtp $^{=2,5}$ -dihydroxyterephthalate).[103] In this material, the $Z\mathbf{n}^{2+}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{D}_{2}$ distance was estimated to be $2.6\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ , a somewhat larger value than those observed for $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ , which likely contributes to the comparatively low initial binding energy of 8.8 kJ mol1.[103] Notably, very short $\mathbf{D}_{2}\mathbf{-D}_{2}$ distances of only $2.85\mathrm{\AA}$ were observed within the first adsorbed layer, suggesting that the presence of unsaturated metal sites can indeed increase the packing efficiency of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ within microporous frameworks relative to even solid hydrogen, which exhibits intermolecular distances of 3.6 L.[103]
In two other experiments, inelastic neutron scattering (INS) spectroscopy was used to prove $\mathbf{Ni}^{2+}{-}\mathbf{D}_{2}$ interactions in the microporous nickel phosphate $\mathrm{Ni}_{20}(\mathrm{OH})_{12}[(\mathrm{HPO}_{4})_{8}-$ $(\mathrm{PO}_{4})_{4}]^{[58]}$ and in the nickel sulfoisophthalate $\mathrm{NaNi}_{3}(\mathrm{OH})$ - $(\mathrm{sip})_{2}$ .[57] As shown in Figure 3, the isophthalate framework has three crystallographically independent nickel atoms, two of which are coordinated by water molecules, which can be evacuated to give unsaturated $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ sites. Although the INS experiments indicated metal– ${\bf\cdot D}_{2}$ interactions that were subsequently attributed to the $\mathbf{Ni}^{2+}$ ions, it is possible that further experiments would also reveal ${\bf D}_{2}$ binding to the $\mathrm{{Na^{+}}}$ ions, which initially possess two terminal water ligands. As shown in Table 1 and discussed in Section 3, however, the $\mathrm{\DeltaNa^{+}{-}H_{2}}$ interaction is expected to be much weaker than the $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}{-}H}_{2}$ interaction, which therefore ought to be almost entirely responsible for the high zero-coverage $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energy of $10.4\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ observed in $\mathrm{NaNi_{3}(O H)(s i p)_{2}}$ .[57]
The relatively large enthalpies of adsorption observed in the aforementioned compounds have positive effects on their hydrogen uptake capacities. For example, $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{3}[(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{4}\mathbf{Cl})_{3}}\end{array}$ - $\mathrm{(btt)_{\mathrm{8}}(C H_{3}O H)_{10}]_{2}}$ exhibits a total $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of $6.9\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 90 bar and $77\mathrm{K}$ , which corresponds to a volumetric capacity of $60\ \mathrm{gL}^{-1}$ , only $11\ \mathrm{gL}^{-1}$ lower than the density of liquid $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ at $20\mathrm{K}$ . As mentioned before, the metal– $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}$ distances in this material are at least $1\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ shorter than typical van der Waals contacts, which are normally greater than $3.3\mathring\mathrm{A}$ . This result suggests that $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules pack more efficiently inside pores lined with unsaturated metal centers, proving that this approach is a key strategy for achieving high volumetric storage density. By comparison, $[\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{dmf})_{6}]_{3}[(\mathrm{Mn}_{4}\mathrm{Cl})_{3}.$ - $\mathrm{(btt)}_{8}(\mathrm{dmf)}_{12}]_{2}$ , the isomorphous framework in which the coordination spheres of all $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{n}}^{2+}$ ions are saturated by DMF molecules and the zero-coverage enthalpy of adsorption is only $7.6\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ , adsorbs a total of only $3.9\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 50 bar and $77\mathrm{K}$ .[55]
The positive influence of the unsaturated metal sites becomes evident especially when comparing the room temperature adsorption capacities of the frameworks in Table 3 with the results obtained for the best metal–organic frameworks without unsaturated metal sites. The current records for lowtemperature $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption in metal–organic frameworks were obtained with $Z\mathrm{n}_{4}\mathrm{O}(1,3,5\$ -benzenetribenzoate) $)_{2}$ and $Z_{\mathrm{{n_{4}O(1,4)}}}$ -benzenedicarboxylate)3, commonly known as MOF-177 and MOF-5, respectively. These compounds adsorb totals of 11 and $9.8~\mathrm{wt}\%$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ at 90 bar and $77\mathrm{K}$ , corresponding to 49 and $64~\mathrm{gL}^{-1}$ , respectively.[66,67] As expected, the total adsorption capacity for MOF-5 increases almost linearly with pressure above 100 bar, and reaches
$11.9\mathrm{wt\%}$ and $79\mathrm{gL}^{-1}$ at 180 bar and $77\mathrm{K}$ , thus exceeding the density of liquid $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ at $20\mathrm{K}$ . However, the low adsorption enthalpy of approximately $5\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ is responsible for a total room-temperature uptake of only $1.4\mathrm{wt\%}$ and $8.1\ \mathrm{gL}^{-1}$ at 90 bar.[67] By comparison, despite exhibiting a BET surface area of only $2057\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , approximately half of the $3800{\mathrm{m}}^{2}{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ value for MOF-5, $\mathrm{Mn}_{3}[(\mathrm{Mn}_{4}\mathrm{Cl})_{3}(\mathrm{btt})_{8}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH})_{10}]_{2}$ displays a total room-temperature capacity of $1.5\mathrm{wt}\%$ under identical conditions.[55] Moreover, the volumetric capacity for the manganese framework is $50\%$ higher than that of MOF-5, and at $12.1\ \mathrm{gL}^{-1}$ it represents a $77\%$ increase over the density of compressed $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ under the same conditions.[68] These results suggest that new materials that combine the advantages of large surface areas and high binding energies are expected to demonstrate even more promising storage properties at room temperature. Such materials could be produced, for example, by using known building units with unsaturated metal centers, such as the $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}$ –paddlewheel and the square-planar $\bf{M n}_{4}C l$ clusters, and extending the length of the bridging ligands. Despite possible complications due to the formation of interpenetrated frameworks, this approach has succeeded in producing isomorphous frameworks with increasing surface areas.[85]
## 5. Strategies for Incorporating Unsaturated Metal Centers in Metal–Organic Frameworks
The most common method for achieving coordinative unsaturation involves the removal of metal-bound volatile species, which typically function as terminal ligands for the metals embedded within the porous framework. Two other methods have also been reported, and they involve either the incorporation of metal species within the organic bridging ligands, or the impregnation of a given framework with excess metal cations.
## 5.1. Metal Building Units with Coordinatively Unsaturated Centers through Solvent Removal
The most widely exploited method thus far involves the synthesis of solvated metal–organic frameworks, from which metal-bound solvent molecules, such as N,N-dimethylformamide, N,N-diethylformamide, water, or methanol, are removed to produce coordinatively unsaturated metal centers. Although a large number of different frameworks have been produced by using this technique, most are based on a relatively small number of metal building units. These building units are either small multinuclear metal clusters or metal chains bridged by carboxylate or tetrazolate groups.
Figure 4 displays the types of unsaturated metal clusters that have been found inside the metal–organic frameworks listed in Table 3. One of the most ubiquitous cluster motifs is the bimetallic tetracarboxylate paddlewheel unit $\{\mathbf{M}_{2}\mathbf{.}$ - $\mathrm{(O_{2}C R)_{4}}\}$ (1), which is frequently formed in reactions involving ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ and $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ cations. Each metal ion in 1 is coordinated by four carboxylate groups and a solvent molecule in a square-pyramidal geometry. Solvent molecules on each of the two ions can be removed to give open metal sites, as observed for example in $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{3}(\mathrm{{btc})_{2}}$ and $\mathbf{Mo}_{3}(\mathrm{btc})_{2}$ . Another common carboxylate-bridged cluster is $\{\mathbf{M}_{3}(\mu_{3}{\bf-O})(\mathbf{O}_{2}{\bf C R})_{6}\}$ (4), an oxocentered trigonal building unit, which in Table 3 is found in frameworks of ${\mathrm{Sc}}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+/3+}$ , $\mathbf{Ni}^{2+}$ , $\mathbf{Al}^{3+}$ , and $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ . These frameworks can be synthesized under conditions mimicking those used for the formation of the geometrically analogous molecular clusters, which are known for an even wider variety of metal combinations.[96] Each metal ion in building unit 4 displays one potential $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding site, as opposed to the linear trinuclear clusters $\{{\bf M}_{3}({\bf O}_{2}{\bf C R})_{6}\}$ (6), in which two of the metal centers each present two possible $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding sites. Cluster 4 and the oxo-centered square-planar cluster $\{\mathbf{M}_{4}(\mu_{4}{\mathbf-}\mathbf{O})\cdot$ - $\left(\mathbf{O}_{2}\mathbf{CR}\right)_{8}\right\}$ (8) have geometrically related tetrazolate-bridged analogues represented by the linear unit $\{\mathbf{M}_{3}(\mathbf{N}_{4}\mathbf{CR})_{6}\}$ (5) and the chloride-centered square-planar unit $\{\mathbf{M}_{4}(\mu_{4}\mathbf{-Cl})(\mathbf{N}_{4}\mathbf{CR})_{8}\}$ (7). The latter is featured in the sodalite-like framework $\mathrm{Mn}_{3}[(\mathrm{Mn}_{4}\mathrm{Cl})_{3}(\mathrm{btt})_{8}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH})_{10}]_{2}$ and its ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ analogue.
Less common are the hydroxo- and bis(m-oxo)-bridged clusters $\{\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mu{\mathrm{-OH}})(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{CR})_{5}\}$ (2) and $\{\mathrm{Fe}_{4}(\mu_{3}{\bf-O})_{2}({\bf O}_{2}{\bf C R})_{8}\}$ (9), which are only featured once each in Table 3. Also encountered only once thus far is the oxo-centered tetrazolate-bridged cluster $\{\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mu_{3}{\bf-O})(\mathrm{N}_{4}\mathrm{CR})_{3}\}$ (3), which was reported only recently in $\mathrm{Cu}_{6}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{tzi})_{3}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})$ , a rare example of a framework built from two types of unsaturated metal clusters. As opposed to the triangular carboxylate unit 4, cluster 3 has only three bridging tetrazolate rings, and each metal displays a trigonal-bipyramidal geometry with two potential $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding sites.
In addition to the frameworks in Table 2, materials based on selected clusters in Figure 4 exhibit some of the highest $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ capacities for metal–organic frameworks. Among the materials that were investigated at high pressure, the isoreticular frameworks ${\mathrm{Cu}}_{2}(\mathrm{bptc})$ , $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{2}(\mathrm{{tptc})}$ , and $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{2}(\mathrm{{qptc})}$ exhibit excess gravimetric $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ capacities (excess capacity $\c=$ uptake due to material only, not including uptake due to compression of gas in the empty volume) of 4.20, 6.06, and $6.07\mathrm{wt}\%$ at 20 bar and 77 K.[85] High excess capacities were also reported for $\mathrm{Cr}_{3}\mathrm{OF}(\mathrm{bdc})_{3}$ and $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{3}(\mathrm{{BPTriC})}$ , which at $77\mathrm{K}$ adsorbed 6.1 and $5.7\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 60 and 45 bar, respectively. The maximal adsorption enthalpies for these compounds, which include presumptive contributions from unsaturated $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ions, respectively, are 10.0 and $7.3\mathrm{kJmol^{-1}}$ and are among the highest known for microporous materials. Strong $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption is also observed in $\mathrm{H}_{2}[\mathrm{Co}_{4}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{tatb})_{8/3}]$ and in $\mathrm{Cu}_{6}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{tzi})_{3}-$ $\left(\mathrm{NO}_{3}\right)$ , for which zero-coverage adsorption enthalpy values of 10.1 and $9.5\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ have been attributed to $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding to unsaturated ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ centers, respectively. Although high-pressure data is not available for these compounds, relatively high capacities of 1.53 and $2.4\mathrm{wt}\%$ were reported at 1 bar and $77\mathrm{K}$ for the cobalt and copper frameworks, respectively. Notable low-pressure capacities were also observed for $[\mathrm{In}_{3}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{abtc})_{1.5}](\mathrm{NO}_{3})$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}\mathrm{O}_{2}(\mathrm{btb})_{8/3}$ , which adsorbed 2.61 and $2.1\mathrm{wt}\%$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , respectively, at around 1 bar and $77\mathrm{K}$ .
The chains shown in Figure 5 constitute the inorganic building units for the remaining materials listed in Table 3. These motifs are far less common than the small multinuclear clusters discussed above. In fact, each of these chains is found in only one framework in Table 3, with the exception of the carboxylate-bridged chain 12, which is featured in both ${\mathrm{Y}}_{2}(\mathrm{pdc})_{3}$ and $\mathrm{Er}_{2}(\mathrm{pdc})_{3}$ . In contrast to the cluster-based materials, which normally display three-dimensional channels and have high surface areas and large micropore volumes, chain-based metal–organic frameworks typically exhibit onedimensional channels that lead to low surface areas and reduced micropore volumes. As such, despite exhibiting a relatively high adsorption enthalpy of $8.8\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ , $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{dhtp})$ shows an $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ capacity of only $2.8~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at 30 bar and $77\mathrm{K}$ , which is still the best value reported thus far for a chain-based microporous framework with coordinatively unsaturated metal sites.
Although themogravimetric analysis and powder X-ray diffraction data suggest that empty coordination sites may become available in the frameworks in Table 3, direct evidence for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding to these materials has not yet been reported, as the high temperature required to evacuate metalbound solvent molecules can lead to loss of long-range ordering and crystallinity. The low thermal stability of most frameworks thereby prevents the use of common neutron diffraction techniques, which could provide direct structural evidence of metal– $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding. For example, although the adsorption enthalpies of 8.8, 8.7, and $8.4\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ reported for $\mathrm{Mn}_{2}(\mathrm{bdt})\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mathrm{bdt})_{3}$ , and $\mathbf{Mn}_{3}(\mathrm{bdt})_{3}$ , respectively, were attributed to $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding to $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{n}}^{2+}$ and $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ sites, the poor crystallinity of these frameworks prevented further studies of the presumed metal– $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding interactions.
In other cases, as for example in $\mathrm{{\bfMg}}_{3}(\mathrm{ndc})_{3}$ and $\mathbf{Mn}(\mathrm{ndc})$ , desolvation is accompanied by rearrangements to different crystalline phases. Powder diffraction patterns for these desolvated phases differ from those of the as-synthesized materials, and unless crystals of the respective compounds remain single upon desolvation, identification of the rearranged structures is often difficult. The development of milder desolvation techniques is therefore necessary to allow further investigation of metal– $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding in known materials. In addition, the synthesis of more thermally robust materials should provide new opportunities to study the metal– $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}$ interaction and ultimately to devise principles for the design of improved hydrogen storage materials.
Cyano-bridged frameworks typically exhibit excellent crystallinity and are thus more amenable to neutron studies.[97] As in the metal–organic frameworks, coordinatively unsaturated metal sites become available in the cyanidebridged materials upon careful evacuation of the metal-bound water molecules. One such example is the Prussian blue analogue $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{2}$ , for which low-temperature neutron diffraction revealed $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}{-}\mathbf{D}_{2}$ interactions at increased ${\bf D}_{2}$ loading.[64] Surprisingly, the related Prussian blue analogue $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{3}[\mathbf{Co}(\mathbf{CN})_{6}]_{2}}\end{array}$ did not exhibit $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{2+}{-}\mathbf{D}_{2}$ interactions even under increased ${\bf D}_{2}$ loadings,[98] in contrast to the aforementioned results, indicating that isostructural sodalite-type compounds bind $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ more strongly to $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{n}}^{2+}$ centers than to $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ .[55,65] The result is in line, however, with previous $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption data for a series of Prussian blue analogues $\mathrm{{\bfM}_{3}[C o(C N)_{6}]}$ , which exhibited maximal adsorption enthalpies of $5.9\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ for $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{2+}$ and $7.4\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ for $\mathbf{M}=$ $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ .[97a] Hydrogen storage measurements for metal–cyanide frameworks with the general formula $\mathrm{A_{2}Z n_{3}[F e(C N)_{6}]_{2}}$ ( $\mathbf{A}=$ alkali metal) have also allowed comparison of the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding strengths of $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}^{+}$ , $\mathrm{Li(H}_{2}\mathrm{O})^{+}$ , $\mathrm{{Na}^{+}}$ , $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ , and $\mathbf{R}\mathbf{b}^{+}$ .[97e] The zerocoverage enthalpy of adsorption in these materials decreased in the order $\mathrm{K^{+}}>\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O^{+}}>\mathrm{Rb^{+}}\approx\mathrm{Li(H_{2}O)^{+}}>\mathrm{Na^{+}}$ and ranged from $9.0\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{K^{+}}$ to $7.7\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{{Na^{+}}}$ .
## 5.2. Incorporating Coordinatively Unsaturated Metal Centers within the Organic Linkers
A second method for introducing unsaturated metal centers within metal–organic frameworks is to attach metal fragments to the organic bridging ligands. This could be accomplished, for example, by employing $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine- $.5{,}5^{\prime}.$ - dicarboxylate $\mathrm{(H_{2}B i p y D C)}$ or similar metal chelating dicarboxylates or ditetrazolates as the bridging ligands. In contrast to the strategy described in Section 5.1, the unsaturated metal centers produced in this manner would not be part of the metal-building unit of a given framework and therefore their incorporation could be accomplished either prior to or after the synthesis of a given framework. At the same time, metal centers introduced by using this method would be more amenable to chemical modifications, which is particularly attractive because it implies that multiple metal– $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding sites could become available by removing, for example, all four carbonyl ligands from a hypothetical bridging ligand $\mathrm{\small{[(BipyDC)M(CO)_{4}]^{2-}}}$ (16; Figure 6). Although unsaturated metal sites have not been obtained yet with this ligand, porous metal–organic frameworks incorporating both metal-free, and metal-ligated BipyD $\displaystyle{\mathfrak{C}}^{2-}$ units have been reported.[99]
Chelated metal centers have also been isolated inside porous frameworks by using porphyrin-[100] and salen-type ligands (salen $\mathbf{\Lambda}=N\mathbf{\mathcal{N}}^{\prime}$ -bis(salicylidene)ethylenediamine),[101] such as the salen– $\cdot\mathrm{Mn}^{3+}$ complex 17 (Figure 6). Complex 17 functions as a bridging ligand in a pillared $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ -based framework,[101b] and although no $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ uptake data is reported for this material, the $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{n}}^{3+}$ ion can function as a Lewis acid catalyst, suggesting that it displays open coordination sites that could lead to strong $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption. These and similar results reported by Suslick and co-workers for porphyrinbased frameworks[100] suggest that a metal–chelate-based strategy could lead to novel materials with interesting $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage properties.
Indeed, an important result in this area was the incorporation of half-sandwich units $\{(\mathrm{bdc})\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{CO})_{3}\}$ (18) inside $\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{bdc})_{3}$ .[102] Evacuation of all three $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{CO}}$ molecules from $\{\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{CO})_{3}\}$ units in $\mathrm{Zn_{4}O[(b d c)C r(C O)_{3}]_{3}}$ was evidenced by thermogravimetric analysis, and framework integrity was confirmed by powder X-ray diffraction. However, a change from colorless to gray indicated the possible aggregation of $\mathrm{Cr}$ atoms at increased temperature, such that the adsorption capacity of this material reached only 0.2 molecules of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ per formula unit. Milder photolysis methods were therefore used to decarbonylate $\mathrm{Zn_{4}O[(b d c)C r(C O)_{3}]_{3}}$ , and infrared spectroscopy showed that $\mathrm{Zn_{4}O[(b d c)C r(C O)_{2}(H_{2})]_{3}}$ and $\mathrm{Zn_{4}O}.$ - $\mathrm{\small{[(bdc)Cr(CO)_{2}(N_{2})]_{3}}}$ could be produced under UV light in atmospheres of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and ${\bf N}_{2}$ , respectively. Unfortunately, the low efficiency of the photolysis in solid-state samples precluded further decarbonylation of these products, such that other, more efficient means to remove carbonyl ligands need to be developed to take advantage of all three metal binding sites on the half-sandwich units. Nevertheless, given that the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energy for the hydrogenated species is expected to be in the vicinity of $60{-}70\ \mathrm{kJmol^{-1}}$ , as measured for $[(\mathrm{C_{6}H_{6}})\mathrm{Cr(CO)}_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2})]^{[26]}$ and $[(\mathrm{C_{6}H_{5}M e})\mathrm{Cr(CO)}_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2})]$ ,[27] these results suggest that an approach involving the functionalization of the organic bridging ligands could produce materials with very high $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ affinity.
## 5.3. Impregnation of Metal–Organic Frameworks with Metal Ions
A very recent development in the area of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage in metal–organic frameworks has been the use of ion exchan$\mathrm{ge}^{[68\mathsf{b}]}$ and metal impregnation techniques for stronger $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding. For example, attempts to exchange the $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{n}}^{2+}$ cations that balance the charge of the anionic framework in $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{3}$ - $[(\mathrm{Mn}_{4}\mathrm{Cl})_{3}(\mathrm{btt})_{8}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH})_{10}]_{2}$ almost invariably resulted in the introduction of extra equivalents of metal chlorides to produce materials of the type $\mathrm{{M}_{3}[(M n_{4}C l)_{3}(b t t)_{8}.}$ - $(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH})_{10}]_{2}{\cdot}x\mathrm{MCl}_{2}$ $\mathrm{TM}=\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ , $\mathbf{Co}^{2+}$ , $\mathbf{Ni}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ , $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ; $x=$ 0–2).[68a] As shown in Table 3, these new materials exhibit a large variation in the zero-coverage $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption enthalpy, ranging from $8.5\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ for the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ -exchanged framework to $10.5\mathrm{kJmol^{-1}}$ for the $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$ -exchanged phase. The latter represents the highest value reported thus far for a microporous metal–organic framework.
Employing a different approach, Mulfort and Hupp used a suspension of Li metal in DMF to reduce $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{ndc})_{2}(\mathrm{diPyNI})$ , a metal–organic framework with a pillared structure.[69] This procedure allowed doping of the as-synthesized material with approximately $5\mathrm{mol}\%$ of $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ cations, resulting in a remarkable increase in the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ adsorption capacity from 0.93 to $1.63\mathrm{~wt.~\%}$ at $77\mathrm{K}$ and 1 atm. The calculated isosteric enthalpy of adsorption also showed an increase from the assynthesized material over the entire $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ loading range. The zero-coverage $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding energy in this $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ -doped material was a modest $6.1\mathrm{\kJmol^{-1}}$ , which is nevertheless in good agreement with previous measurements for $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ -exchanged zeolites.[11–13] Despite the scarcity of examples that demonstrate the metal impregnation strategy, the two examples reported thus far are encouraging and suggest that other materials can be modified in a similar manner to produce microporous frameworks with an enhanced hydrogen affinity.
## 6. Conclusion and Outlook
Microporous metal–organic frameworks are promising hydrogen storage materials, and the isolation of unsaturated metal ions can be used as a systematic way to increase the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ binding affinity. Although many known frameworks may display metal– $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}$ interactions, very few experiments have been performed to test this assumption. Critical areas that are likely to produce better results or improve on the ones reported so far are the elucidation of milder methods to desolvate metals within the pores and the synthesis of more robust frameworks that can maintain crystallinity during the thermal evacuation of metal-bound solvent molecules. Possible desolvation strategies for thermally sensitive frameworks may include microwave or photolytic evacuation methods. In turn, the development of new ligands with metal-binding groups that form stronger metal–ligand bonds should lead to materials with increased thermal stability. Another possible strategy that could yield metal–organic frameworks with increased $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ affinity involves the incorporation of a larger concentration of charged sites within the pores. This could be achieved either by the use of multi-anionic bridging ligands, which should increase the number of metal atoms per formula unit, or by the incorporation of negative charges, which can also interact electrostatically with the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ quadrupole.
Overall, very encouraging results have been reported in a relatively short time, and a few new strategies to obtain unsaturated metal centers were developed only within the last year. Moreover, some of the results reported thus far show that metal–organic frameworks can meet most of the 2010 DoE targets on a materials basis when operating at $77\mathrm{K}$ . These allow researchers in the area to be optimistic when faced with the challenge of increasing the $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ binding energy to produce a hydrogen storage system that will ultimately function near ambient temperature.
## Addendum
Two very recent reports have demonstrated strong interactions between $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and exposed metal sites within metal–organic frameworks. An isosteric heat of adsorption of $12.3\mathrm{kJmol}^{-1}$ was reported for $\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mathrm{bdc})_{3}[\mathrm{Cu(pyen)}]$ $\mathrm{(pyenH}_{2}=5$ -methyl-4-oxo-1,4-dihydropyridine-3-carbaldehyde)[104] and an initial adsorption enthalpy of $13.5\mathrm{kJmol^{-1}}$ was established for $\mathrm{\bfNi}_{2}(\mathrm{dhtp})$ by using variable-temperature infrared spectroscopy.[105]
